# Types

`strict` TypeScript, plus `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`,
`exactOptionalPropertyTypes`, `noImplicitOverride` and
`verbatimModuleSyntax`.

- No `any`.
- No `as` casts to force a shape into place.
- No `@ts-expect-error` in `src/`.
- Type-only imports use `import type` (the lint enforces it).

If the types fight you, the model is wrong — change the model, in a card that
owns it. Do not cast your way past a contract; the contract is the product.

Validation at the boundary is the exception that proves this: `parse()` and
`loadBundle()` take `unknown` and narrow it honestly, one field at a time.
That is not a cast, that is the check.
